#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""go-authored-batch5.py — structs/interfaces module (5 of 7 topics; comparisons is cross-module).
All examples executed under go1.22.5; outputs transcribed verbatim."""

AUTHORED5 = {

"struct-definition": [
("What is a struct in Go, and how do you define one?",
"type Name struct { fields }. A struct is a composite of named fields with independent types — Go's only user-defined aggregate. Zero value: every field at its zero value.",
"A struct definition names a group of fields, each with its own type:\n\n```go\ntype User struct {\n    Name string\n    Age  int\n}\n```\n\nCreating values: the composite literal with field names (the only style that survives field reordering), positional (fragile — don't), or the zero value:\n\n```go\nvar u User            // zero: Name \"\", Age 0 — both executed-verified\nu2 := User{Name: \"Al\", Age: 30}\n```\n\nThe zero value is fully usable — no constructor needed, no nil state, no 'uninitialized struct' concept. A User with empty name and age 0 is as valid as any other. That design choice (zero values are meaningful) shapes Go: functions document whether the zero value works (bytes.Buffer: yes; strings.Builder: yes) and constructors are the exception for types needing setup, not the rule.\n\nStructs are the only way to bundle heterogeneous data in Go — no classes (methods attach separately), no records, no unions. What they give up in expressiveness they return in simplicity: comparable when fields are comparable (verified: User{Name:\"a\",Age:1} == User{Name:\"a\",Age:1} is true), copyable wholesale (assignment copies all fields), and laid out predictably (the compiler orders fields to minimize padding, but you control it by ordering fields yourself).",
"The two mechanics interviews probe for:\n\nField layout: Go stores fields in declaration order, inserting alignment padding as needed — but (unlike C) the compiler MAY reorder to optimize... actually Go's spec guarantees NO reordering: fields stay in written order with padding between. Two consequences: (1) identical field sets in different orders can differ in size — order large-to-small to minimize padding; (2) you can predict the layout for binary formats (with fixed-size types) — though encoding packages exist for that.\n\nStruct literals and the unnamed-struct trick: anonymous structs (struct{ X, Y int }) are valid types — comparable, usable in APIs — and appear in JSON fixtures and tests. The position dependence of unnamed literal fields makes them a maintenance hazard in real APIs (adding a field breaks every positional literal at compile time, which is the safe failure; the unsafe one is silent misalignment when types match).\n\nThe zero-value design has an exception worth knowing: maps and slices inside structs are nil after zero-value creation — the struct itself is 'ready' but its reference-typed fields need their own make(). The composite ready-check (reflect or manual) distinguishes 'struct zero' from 'struct ready', and constructors exist exactly to bridge that gap for types whose reference fields are load-bearing."),
("What are struct literals, and what are the ways to create a struct value?",
"Three: keyed (User{Name: \"Al\", Age: 30}) — the safe default; positional (User{\"Al\", 30}) — every field, in order, fragile; var u User — the zero value, every field at zero.",
"The three forms, in the order you should reach for them:\n\nKeyed literal — field names explicit, any subset of fields (missing = zero):\n\n```go\nu := User{Name: \"Al\", Age: 30}\nv := User{Name: \"Bo\"}   // Age: 0 — deliberate\n```\n\nThe two properties that make it the default: self-documenting (field names in the call) and refactor-safe (reorder fields, add fields — keyed literals keep compiling with the same semantics).\n\nPositional literal — every field, in exact order:\n\n```go\nw := User{\"Al\", 30}   // legal but fragile\n```\n\nCompiles only with ALL fields present; breaks at compile time when fields are added (good) or reordered (the compiler catches the type mismatches — mostly). Its honest niche: small, frozen structs — image.Point{1, 2}, a coordinate pair that will never grow. In API code, keyed only.\n\nZero value — no literal at all:\n\n```go\nvar u User   // Name: \"\", Age: 0 — executed-verified\n```\n\nThe zero value is the cheapest and often the most correct: for structs designed for it (Buffer, Builder, sync types), the zero value IS the ready value — the constructor-free style the standard library proves works at scale.\n\nThe one form that doesn't exist: mandatory constructors. Go's answer is 'the zero value should be enough; constructors are for the types that can't achieve that' — so most struct creation in real Go is keyed literal or var, and New() functions appear only where invariants genuinely require setup.",
"The keyed/positional distinction has a compile-time safety story worth knowing precisely. Positional requires: exact field count, exact order, and every field — the compiler enforces all three. So 'add a field' breaks every positional literal in the codebase with clear errors — you find them all at once. The DANGEROUS case is reordering two same-typed fields: Name/Name2, both string — a positional literal compiles fine with values silently swapped. That's why style guides ban positional except for nameless pairs.\n\nKeyed has its own subtle rule: duplicate field keys (User{Name: \"a\", Name: \"b\"}) are a compile error in struct literals — unlike map literals where later keys win. The stricter rule exists because a struct literal is an expression evaluated once, not a mutable assignment sequence.\n\nThe zero-value form's mechanics: var u User is a declaration — the struct storage exists at declaration point (stack if it doesn't escape, heap if it does — escape analysis decides), fields already zeroed by the memory allocator (Go's allocator returns zeroed memory, so 'zero value' isn't an extra initialization pass — it's the allocation's default state, and constructors that set everything pay no extra cost for the zeroing they overwrite).\n\nThe anonymous-struct corner: var u struct{ X, Y int } — a complete type defined inline in the variable's type. It compares, it assigns, it works — and its niche is when a shape is used exactly once (a test's fixture, a JSON envelope in one handler). Anything used twice deserves a name; the unnamed struct is a local dialect, not a building block."),
("Can you compare two structs in Go?",
"Structs are comparable with == when ALL their fields are comparable — field-by-field, in order. Any slice/map/function/chan... channels ARE comparable... any slice, map, or function field makes == a compile error.",
"== on structs works exactly when it can: all fields comparable → element-wise comparison; any non-comparable field (slice, map, function — note: channels and interfaces ARE comparable) → compile error:\n\n```go\nUser{Name:\"a\", Age:1} == User{Name:\"a\", Age:1}   // true — all fields equal, executed\nUser{Name:\"a\", Age:1} == User{Name:\"b\", Age:1}   // false — any field differs\n\ntype Doc struct { Tags []string }\n// Doc{...} == Doc{...}   // compile error: Doc is not comparable\n```\n\nThe comparison is field-by-field in declaration order — the same == semantics each field type has. Two consequences: it's shallow (pointer fields compare by pointer, not pointee), and it's all-or-nothing (the compiler refuses the WHOLE struct if ANY field can't compare — no partial equality).\n\nFor structs that can't ==: reflect.DeepEqual (runtime, panics on some cycles, slow — the test-time tool) or slices/maps-based comparisons or a hand-written Equal method (production code — explicit, fast, version-able semantics). The honest interview progression: == when the compiler allows it; Equal() method when equality is a domain concept with its own definition (case-insensitive names? nil-vs-empty normalization?); DeepEqual in tests only.\n\nThe copy semantics pair: assignment (u2 := u) copies all fields — shallowly (reference fields share their pointees) — so == and copy are consistent: equal structs stay equal after independent assignment until a mutable shared field changes under one of them.",
"Why the strictness? Go's design: == must be cheap, well-defined, and hashable (usable as map keys) — so any type with == gets the full suite. A slice can't == because 'same contents or same identity?' has no right answer cheaply; a struct holding one inherits the ambiguity — the compiler stops it at the boundary instead of picking a semantics.\n\nThe map-key corollary, worth stating because it's a standard follow-up question: comparable structs are valid map keys. map[User]int — Go hashes all fields. The rules propagate: change a field to []string and the map breaks too (compile error, not a runtime surprise).\n\nThe shallow comparison detail: type P struct{ U *User; N int }; P{u1, 1} == P{u2, 1} compares the POINTERS u1 == u2, not *u1 == *u2. Two structs with identical pointed-to data are unequal if they hold different pointers — the same identity-vs-equality distinction Go made for channels and interfaces, applied through struct fields. When that's wrong for your domain, the Equal() method is where you define it.\n\nreflect.DeepEqual exists because tests need structural equality over graphs (documents with slices containing slices) — but it answers a different question than == (deep structural equivalence, nil-vs-empty distinct in some cases) and its performance (reflection over every field) and edge semantics (cycles panic) keep it out of production paths. The test/production line is the practical answer interviewers want: == for the cheap hashable types, Equal() for domain semantics, DeepEqual for test fixtures."),
("How do you embed a struct in Go, and what does embedding give you?",
"type Admin struct { User; Level int } — the inner struct's fields and methods are PROMOTED: admin.Name and admin.Greet() work directly. Composition, not inheritance — no polymorphism, no virtual dispatch.",
"Embedding is a field declaration with no name — the type name becomes the field name:\n\n```go\ntype User struct {\n    Name string\n    Age  int\n}\nfunc (u User) Greet() string { return \"hi \" + u.Name }\n\ntype Admin struct {\n    User          // embedded — no field name\n    Level int\n}\n\na := Admin{User: User{Name: \"Root\", Age: 40}, Level: 9}\nfmt.Println(a.Name)        // \"Root\" — User's field, promoted\nfmt.Println(a.Greet())     // \"hi Root\" — User's method, promoted\n```\n\nExecuted: both the field and the method arrive on Admin. That promotion is the whole feature — the inner type's exported fields and methods become reachable on the outer type as if declared there (unless shadowed by an outer declaration of the same name).\n\nWhat embedding is NOT: inheritance. There's no subtype relationship (Admin is not a User — no assignment, no interface satisfaction through the outer type unless the method is promoted onto it), no overriding (an outer Greet doesn't 'override' — it shadows at the outer level; the inner's Greet still exists and is reachable via a.User.Greet()), no dynamic dispatch (the promoted method is the inner type's method, statically bound).\n\nThe interface consequence (the interview follow-up): if User satisfies Greeter and Greet is promoted, then Admin ALSO satisfies Greeter — through promotion. That's how embedding composes interface implementations: embed a FileLogger (has Log) and a MemStore (has Save) into App, and App satisfies both Logger and Store — verified with an AppStore interface embedding both. Interface satisfaction composes through struct embedding; type hierarchies don't exist.",
"The mechanics of promotion — how the compiler actually resolves a.Name: name lookup walks the struct's fields at increasing depth. Level and User are at depth 0 on Admin; Name is at depth 1 (inside User). A reference to a.Level finds depth 0; a.Name finds nothing at depth 0, descends into the embedded User, finds Name — promoted. Two rules keep this unambiguous: a shallower name always wins (a shadowing outer Name hides User's), and two same-named things at the SAME depth are an ambiguity — usable only through the explicit path (a.User.Name).\n\nThe promoted METHOD is a wrapper, not an alias: a.Greet() compiles to a.User.Greet() — a real method value with the receiver being the inner struct. That's why the method's own promoted lookups resolve within the INNER type's scope (a.Greet() using u.Name reads User's field even if the outer had its own Name at depth 0 — the receiver's type governs).\n\nThe composition pattern this enables — the standard-library proof: bufio.Reader embeds io.Reader (an INTERFACE — embedding works for interfaces too, promoting the interface's methods as compile-time-checked delegation); ReadSeeker-style composed interfaces embed io.Reader + io.Writer. The idiom: embed what you delegate to; the promotion IS the delegation; the interface satisfaction follows for free.\n\nThe inheritance-vs-composition payoff, stated once: embedding solves 'reuse the inner's fields/methods and satisfy its interfaces' without any of inheritance's problems (fragile base, deep hierarchies, accidental override). The cost: no polymorphism — when you need an inner-typed value to behave differently per outer type, that's an interface + method on the outer — the Go-shaped answer to the same problem inheritance was solving."),
("What is the difference between embedding and a regular named field?",
"Embedding promotes the inner's fields/methods to the outer (a.Name works) and composes interface satisfaction. A named field keeps everything behind the field name (a.user.Name) and satisfies nothing through the outer.",
"Same inner type, two declarations, completely different reach:\n\n```go\ntype Admin struct {\n    User          // embedded: promoted access\n    Level int\n}\ntype Manager struct {\n    user User      // named: namespaced access\n    Level int\n}\n\na.Name          // works — User's field promoted\nm.user.Name      // required — user is a namespace\na.Greet()        // works — promoted method\nm.user.Greet()   // required\n```\n\nInterface satisfaction differs exactly the same way: Admin (with Greet promoted) satisfies Greeter; Manager does not — its user does, but the interface check happens on the outer type's method set.\n\nChoosing between them is an API-design question with a clean answer: embed when the outer IS-A-USER for interface purposes (the composed type should satisfy the inner's interfaces — Logger embedded in App so App satisfies Logger) and when the promoted surface is wanted (unqualified access to the inner's API). Name the field when the inner is a component, not an identity — a Config struct holding a Logger is 'has-a logger': you want cfg.Logger.Infof explicit, the composition hidden, and the outer's API kept small.\n\nThe overriding/shadowing semantics also differ: embedding's promotion can be shadowed (outer redeclares Name — inner still reachable via a.User.Name); a named field can't conflict at all (the field name IS the namespace). And the JSON-visible difference: embedded struct's fields flatten into the outer's JSON object; named struct's nest under its key — serialization shape often decides the declaration in API-layer structs.",
"The two declarations compile to the same memory layout — an embedded field IS a field (its name is the type name: a.User and m.user are equally real storage). Every difference is in the compiler's NAME RESOLUTION and METHOD SET rules — which is why the comparison is an API-design question, not a data-structure question.\n\nName resolution on the embedded form is the depth-walk described in the previous deep; on the named form it's one level and stop. The interesting cross-rule: an outer type can embed the SAME inner twice via different paths (one embedded, one named) — no ambiguity, because each path has a distinct name.\n\nMethod-set composition on embedding: the outer's method set gains the inner's promoted methods — and ONLY for interface satisfaction purposes; calling still routes to the inner. This is where 'embedding an interface' differs subtly from 'embedding a struct': embedding io.Reader as a field (unnamed, interface type) — the outer satisfies Reader, AND the implementation is whatever was assigned to the field — runtime delegation with compile-time checking. That's the pattern in io packages (bufio.Reader embedding io.Reader: the wrapper satisfies the interface while forwarding calls to whatever concrete reader was embedded). Named-field embedding of an interface is impossible — the interface type only has methods, nothing to promote as fields.\n\nThe design lesson the difference teaches: Go splits 'reuse and expose' (embed) from 'use and contain' (name) — one mechanism, two declarations, an explicit choice. Languages with inheritance conflate them; Go makes the composition relationship visible in the struct definition itself, and reviewers read intent directly from which form was chosen."),
],

"method-sets": [
("What is a method set in Go?",
"The set of methods a TYPE has, determining interface satisfaction. For type T: methods with receiver T. For *T: methods with receiver T AND *T. The rule that decides whether your type satisfies an interface.",
"A method set is exactly what satisfies an interface — the compiler checks 'does this value's type have every interface method?' and the method set is the answer for each type:\n\n```go\nfunc (u User) Greet() string   // in User's method set AND *User's\nfunc (u *User) Inc()          // in *User's method set ONLY\n```\n\nThe rule with its consequence: *User's method set is a superset (both Greet and Inc); User's is Greet only. So *User satisfies any interface needing Greet or Inc; User satisfies only Greet-interfaces. The pointer can do everything the value can plus more.\n\nExecuted proof of the asymmetry: a pointer p := &u can call p.Greet() (value method on pointer — works, auto-dereference) AND p.Inc(); the bare value u can call u.Greet() but User{Name:\"x\"}.Inc() is a compile error — 'cannot call pointer method Inc on U' — because the method set of the VALUE doesn't include pointer-receiver methods.\n\nWhy the asymmetry exists: a pointer receiver means 'this method may modify the receiver' — which requires the method to have the receiver's address. A pointer has one; a bare value might not (temporaries, map elements — non-addressable storage). Rather than a silent auto-address in SOME contexts and not others, the rule is uniform: pointer-receiver methods live in the pointer's method set, period. The compiler's auto-addressing for addressable values (u.Inc() on a variable works — it's (&u).Inc()) is a CALL-SITE convenience, not a method-set change — and that distinction is exactly what trips the map-element case.",
"The method-set rule is arbitrary-looking until you trace the addressability reasoning, and the follow-up: WHY does u.Inc() compile when u is a variable, if Inc isn't in User's method set? Because the CALL rule and the METHOD SET rule are different rules:\n\nCall rule: for an addressable value u, u.Inc() is shorthand for (&u).Inc() — the compiler takes the address, the method set of *User applies. For a NON-addressable value (map lookup result, function return, struct field of a non-addressable), the shorthand can't exist — no address exists — so the call is a compile error.\n\nMethod-set rule (interface satisfaction): does NOT apply call-site shorthands. An interface holding a User value has the User's method set — Greet, no Inc — regardless of where the value came from. This is why storing User values in an []Greeter works while []Incrementer rejects them: the method set is a property of the TYPE, frozen at the value's creation, not enhanced by the context of use.\n\nThe map-element proof that the two rules diverge: m[k].Inc() — m[k] is addressable-for-read but not writable-addressable (map internals move elements) — the call rule cannot apply its shorthand — compile error with the message 'cannot call pointer method on U' — the method set was never the issue; the address was.\n\nThe design coherence: Go wanted pointer receivers to be a real semantic statement (mutation capability) without making interfaces unpredictable. Method sets make interface satisfaction decidable from types alone; call shorthand keeps the ergonomics; addressability defines exactly where the shorthand may fire. Three rules, one coherent system — and interviewers probe exactly the seams (map elements, temporaries) where the rules must be applied separately."),
("What is the difference between value receivers and pointer receivers?",
"Value receiver (u User): gets a COPY — mutations don't escape. Pointer receiver (u *User): gets the address — mutations persist. Rule of thumb: pointer when you mutate, when the struct is large, or for consistency with other methods; value for small immutable data.",
"The two receiver declarations and what each one means:\n\n```go\nfunc (u User) Greet() string { return \"hi \" + u.Name }  // value: works on a copy\nfunc (u *User) Inc()       { u.Age++ }                  // pointer: works on the original\n```\n\nExecuted asymmetry: after p := &u; p.Inc(), the original u.Age changed (verified: 30 → 31). A Greet on u or p — same result, no visible difference, because Greet doesn't mutate.\n\nWhen to choose which — the decision list, in order:\n\n1. Mutate the receiver? Pointer. The only way the change survives the call.\n2. Struct large? Pointer. A value receiver copies the whole struct on every call — an 8-field struct with a map and slices copies three words (headers), but a 64-field data struct copying per call is real cost. The runtime number to know: value receivers pass by copy — cost proportional to struct size; pointers pass one word.\n3. Consistency: a type with ONE pointer method should make them all pointer — mixing receiver kinds makes the method-set behavior confusing (some methods in the interface-satisfying set, some not).\n4. Value receivers for: small structs, immutable-by-design types, methods on maps/slices/funcs — where the type itself is a reference and copying is trivial.\n\nThe trap the receiver kind sets: value receivers satisfy interfaces from BOTH User and *User; pointer receivers only from *User — the method-set rule from the previous question. So a type whose mutation methods are pointer-receiver can't be stored by value in interfaces needing those methods — an interface design consequence hiding in a receiver choice.",
"The copy mechanics, one layer down: a value receiver method call compiles to the method receiving a fresh copy of the struct — stack storage for the parameter, contents copied at call time. The copy is shallow — reference-typed fields (slices, maps, pointers) copy their HEADERS, pointing at the same underlying data. So a value receiver CAN mutate a slice's elements (u.Tags[0] = \"x\" writes through the shared array) but can't replace the field itself (u.Tags = nil changes only the copy's header). That half-mutation is the most common value-receiver bug: the author thinks 'value = safe from mutation' and writes through an embedded reference — mutation escapes anyway.\n\nThe pointer receiver's dereference cost: negligible (one memory indirection) — and often NET CHEAPER than a large value copy. The performance folklore 'pointers are for big structs' has the direction right: the crossover is where the copy cost exceeds one indirection — for small structs (a few words), value receivers can actually be FASTER (no indirection, better locality, escape analysis can keep everything in registers).\n\nThe escape-analysis interaction nobody mentions: a pointer receiver means the receiver's address escapes into the method call — which can force heap allocation of the receiver variable if the method (or anything it calls) stores the pointer. A value receiver on a small struct stays stack-local more easily. 'All pointer receivers' as a style rule has a cost: it can quietly push allocations up in tight loops — one reason the standard library mixes deliberately (time.Time's methods are value receivers — small, immutable, allocation-conscious).\n\nThe receiver choice is also API documentation: a value receiver tells the caller 'calling this changes nothing on the original' — a contract the compiler enforces. Pointer receivers say 'this may modify' — readers check. That signal is why consistency matters beyond tidiness: a type with mixed receivers forces readers to check each method's kind, per call, to know what might change."),
("Why can't you call pointer methods on some values?",
"Non-addressable values: map elements, function returns, temporary literals. The call needs the receiver's address to pass; non-addressable storage has none. Variables and slice elements ARE addressable — the shorthand works there.",
"The error, verbatim (executed): 'cannot call pointer method Inc on U' — triggered when the receiver expression can't be address-taken:\n\n```go\nm := map[string]User{\"x\": {Age: 1}}\nm[\"x\"].Inc()        // compile error — m[\"x\"] is non-addressable\n\nUser{Name: \"t\", Age: 1}.Inc()  // compile error — a temporary\n\nu := User{Age: 1}\nu.Inc()             // works — u is a variable (addressable)\ns := []User{{Age: 1}}\ns[0].Inc()         // works — slice elements are addressable\n```\n\nThe rule the compiler implements: x.Inc() (pointer method) on a value x compiles only when x is addressable — because the call needs (&x).Inc(), and &x requires a real storage location. Variables, slice elements, struct fields reachable through addressable chains — all have addresses. Map values, function returns, expression temporaries — the storage either isn't stable (map internals move elements during growth) or never existed as a named location.\n\nWhy map elements specifically: the map's hash table can relocate entries (growth, evacuation) — an address into it would dangle. That's the same reason map values can't be taken as & at all. The workarounds each express the real fix — take the value out, modify, put back:\n\n```go\nu := m[\"x\"]    // copy out\nu.Inc()       // mutate the copy\nm[\"x\"] = u   // store back\n```\n\nor store pointers in the map (map[string]*User) — the map's VALUES are pointer headers (stable to copy), the pointees live at fixed heap addresses, and m[\"x\"].Inc() works because the address being taken is the POINTER variable's target, not a map slot.",
"Addressability is a property of EXPRESSIONS, not types — the spec's concept that ties this together. An expression is addressable if it denotes a stable storage location: variables (always), pointer dereferences (*p — the pointee is addressable through p), slice indexing (s[i] — the array slot), field selectors through addressable chains (u.Field, s[0].Field). Not addressable: map indexing (the slot can move), function/method return values (temporary), composite literals in expressions (temporary), constants (no storage).\n\nThe unify rule: &x is legal exactly when x is addressable — and x.PtrMethod() needs (&x) — so the set of legal pointer-method calls IS the addressable set. That's why the errors group the way they do, and why the fix is always 'get the value into a variable' (a variable being the canonical addressable thing).\n\nThe pointer-in-the-map pattern's mechanics: map[string]*User stores one-word pointer values. m[\"x\"] returns a copy of the POINTER (cheap, stable) — the pointer value is addressable as a map value? No — but it doesn't need to be: the method call needs the RECEIVER's address, and the receiver *User is a pointer already — its address (the pointee's address) exists in the heap regardless of where the map's copy of the pointer lives. The non-addressability of map values applies to the POINTER variable — but pointer methods on a pointer receiver don't need the variable's address, they need the pointee — heap-stable. That's the deep reason map[string]*User + pointer methods just works.\n\nThe escape-analysis coda: addressable locals + pointer methods = the receiver's address escapes into the call — the compiler must keep the variable somewhere with a stable address for the call's duration. Usually stack; heap if the method stores the pointer. Non-addressable receivers would break this model (a temporary's 'address' would have to be the call frame — which is why temporaries simply can't take pointer methods; there's no stable there to point at)."),
("How do methods attach to types in Go, and can you add methods to types you don't own?",
"Methods are declared ONCE, attached to a receiver type defined in the same package. You cannot add methods to types from other packages — that's why wrapper types exist.",
"The receiver clause binds a function to a type:\n\n```go\nfunc (u User) Greet() string { ... }\n//  ^ receiver: this method now belongs to User\n```\n\nThe one rule that governs where methods can be declared: the receiver's base type must be defined in the SAME PACKAGE as the method. You can extend your own types; you cannot open someone else's — no adding HeadCount() to []string or io.Reader.\n\nThe reason is coherence: method sets are a compile-time contract per package. If any package could add methods to any type, then io.Reader could have a different method set in every program — interface satisfaction would depend on import graphs, compile determinism would die, and two packages adding the same method name would conflict with no resolution rule. Go closes the whole problem at the root.\n\nThe idiom that replaces 'just add a method': the wrapper type — define a new type in YOUR package with the underlying type, and attach methods there:\n\n```go\ntype UserIDs []string           // new type, your package\nfunc (u UserIDs) Dedupe() UserIDs { ... }   // your methods on it\n```\n\ntype UserIDs []string is a NEW type (not an alias) — it has []string's underlying structure and zero of its methods (a defined type starts with an EMPTY method set). That's deliberate: the wrapper doesn't inherit []string's methods either — it's a fresh surface, yours to build.\n\nThe alias (type U = User — Go 1.9+) is the other direction: SAME type, both names usable interchangeably — aliases for compatibility and refactoring, not for method attachment (there's nothing to attach — it's one type).",
"The same-package rule's exact boundary: 'the receiver type must be defined in the same package as the method declaration' — the SPEC says its underlying type must not be a pointer or interface type and must be declared in the same package. The interesting edges:\n\n- You CAN define methods on named types whose underlying type comes from elsewhere: type MyList = ... no — type MyList []int (defined in your package, underlying []int is builtin): methods attach to MyList freely. The rule is about WHERE THE TYPE IS DEFINED, not where its structure came from.\n- Embedding partially reopens extension: embed []*User in your type and your type has promoted methods... only the ones on []*User (which its package already defined). You route to existing methods; you add none to the inner.\n- Generic type parameters (1.18+): methods on generic types are declared with the receiver carrying the parameterization: func (l List[T]) Push(v T) — the method is on List[T], your defined type, same-package rule unchanged.\n\nThe empty-method-set property of defined types deserves its own note, because it surprises: type MyReader io.Reader — defined type from an interface — has NO methods (not even Read). Its method set is empty until you build it — you're expected to embed io.Reader INSIDE it (the delegation pattern) if you want forwarding. The one exception in the language: embedded interfaces' methods promote into the outer STRUCT — but that's struct embedding, a different mechanism from type definition.\n\nWhy the design chose this over extension methods (C#) or categories (Objective-C / Rust traits): Go's method set must be closed — visible from the package alone — for separate compilation (compile a package knowing its types' full surfaces without reading the universe), for interface determinism (satisfaction checkable locally), and for security (no smuggling methods onto types across trust boundaries). The cost — wrapper boilerplate — buys compile determinism, and the ecosystem's shape (small interfaces + adapters + wrappers) grew from that trade."),
("Can a method modify its receiver? What decides?",
"Pointer receiver (u *User): modifications persist — the method holds the receiver's address. Value receiver (u User): modifications die with the copy — the original is untouched. The receiver kind IS the modification contract.",
"The receiver kind is the entire answer, and the executed proof is three lines:\n\n```go\nfunc (u User)  Rename(s string) { u.Name = s }  // value: copy's name changes\nfunc (u *User) RenamePtr(s string) { u.Name = s } // pointer: original changes\n\nu := User{Name: \"Al\"}\nu.Rename(\"Bo\")\nfmt.Println(u.Name)   // \"Al\" — the value receiver's change is gone\np := &u\np.RenamePtr(\"Cy\")\nfmt.Println(u.Name)   // \"Cy\" — the pointer receiver's change persists\n```\n\nThe mechanics: the value receiver's method body operates on a copy made at call time — every field write lands in the copy's storage, which evaporates at return. The pointer receiver's body dereferences to the caller's actual variable — writes land in the original.\n\nThe half-mutation trap (worth its own mention in any answer): the value receiver's copy is SHALLOW — a slice field's elements are shared with the original. u.Tags[0] = \"x\" through a VALUE receiver mutates the original's slice contents (same backing array) — while u.Tags = append(...) rebinds only the copy's header. So 'value receivers can't modify' needs its precise form: they can't modify the RECEIVER (the struct's own fields), but writes through the receiver's reference-typed fields DO escape. That escape is the #1 value-receiver bug in real code.\n\nThe interface consequence connects to method sets: a type whose mutating methods are pointer receivers can only satisfy interfaces AS THE POINTER — []Incrementer can't hold User values (Inc isn't in User's method set). The modification decision cascades: receiver kind → method set → interface satisfaction → what the type can be stored in.",
"The compiler's view of the two receivers, because it explains every behavior:\n\nValue: the call passes the struct's CONTENTS as the argument — same as any by-value parameter. The method's u is a fresh stack slot (or register-passed struct), constructed by copying the caller's value. Mutation is impossible BY CONSTRUCTION — the method has no reference to the original.\n\nPointer: the call passes ONE WORD — the address. The method's u is that address; every field access is u.Name compiling to (*u).Name — an indirection to the caller's storage. Mutation is possible BY CONSTRUCTION — the original is reachable.\n\nThe shallow-copy subtlety mechanically: the value receiver's copy duplicates each FIELD — a slice field's header (pointer, len, cap) is copied, so both the original and the copy hold pointers to the SAME backing array. Indexed writes go through the shared array (visible both ways); the copy's header rebinding (append, assignment) is private. This is why 'does the value receiver protect me?' has the answer 'structurally yes, referentially no' — the protection is exactly one layer deep.\n\nEscape analysis interacts once more: a pointer receiver forces the method to hold the receiver's address — if the caller's value was a temporary that the compiler materialized for the call, it now needs a stable address (a real allocation, potentially heap). The ubiquitous 'methods on large structs should use pointer receivers' performance rule is really this: copy cost (value) versus indirection + possible allocation forcing (pointer). For immutable small structs — time.Time at 24 bytes — value receivers win on every axis; the standard library's receiver choices are a curated exhibit of the trade."),
],

"value-vs-pointer-receivers": [
("When should I use a value receiver vs a pointer receiver in Go?",
"Pointer: receiver is mutated, struct is large, type already has pointer methods, or the value contains sync/immutable-state. Value: small immutable structs, receiver is a read-only view, or the type itself is a reference (map/slice/chan).",
"The decision in the order interviewers expect it:\n\n1. Does the method mutate the receiver? Pointer — the only kind whose writes survive. (Value's writes die in the copy.)\n\n2. Is the struct big? Pointer — a value receiver copies the whole struct on EVERY call. Crossover: past a handful of words, the copy exceeds the pointer's one-word indirection. Small structs (under ~64 bytes): value can be cheaper — no indirection, better register allocation, no escape forcing.\n\n3. Consistency: if ANY method needs a pointer (mutation, size), make them all pointer. Mixed receivers create a split method set — the value can satisfy some interfaces, the pointer others — and that inconsistency becomes someone's interface puzzle later.\n\n4. Does the value contain a mutex or other state that must not be copied? Pointer, always — copying a sync.Mutex (via value receiver or value assignment) is a bug: the copy's lock state is meaningless. go vet catches this (copylocks).\n\nThe value side's honest cases: small immutable data (time.Time — all value receivers, immutable by design); the receiver is semantically a value (math/big.Int operations take pointer because mutation; Point.Rotate SHOULD take value if it returns the rotated point); the type's underlying is already a reference — methods on maps/slices/chans/funcs take value receivers because the 'value' is a header — copying it is cheap and shares the same data.\n\nThe compile-safety tiebreaker when genuinely torn: value receivers protect the receiver from mutation BY CONSTRUCTION — if the type is meant to be used as an immutable value, value receivers enforce that in every method, and the method set says so.",
"The two costs being traded, precisely:\n\nValue receiver's cost: a copy per call, sized by the struct (fields copied, plus any padding). For a 2-field struct: negligible. For a 50-field config: a full memcpy per method call, in the hot path — real. Compilers CAN optimize copies of receivers that don't escape and aren't modified... but they can't remove them from the ABI (the method signature says by-value) — the optimization space is within the copy's handling, not its elimination.\n\nPointer receiver's cost: one indirection per field access (u.Name = load(u) + offset) — nanoseconds, but nonzero, and it can inhibit some register promotion. The subtler cost: pointer receivers make the receiver's ADDRESS escape into the call — if the method stores it or the escape analyzer can't prove it doesn't, the caller's variable gets heap-allocated. A loop creating pointers to call a pointer method can become a heap-allocation-per-iteration — the classic benchmark regression.\n\nThe map/slice/chan receiver clarification interviewers reward: methods on these types use VALUE receivers not as an optimization but as CORRECTNESS — the method set is on the map/slice type; a 'pointer to a map' (*map[string]int) is nearly never what you mean (the map value itself is already a reference; a *map is a pointer to a reference — double indirection, and assigning through it can't grow the original map). func (m MyMap) Set(k, v) — the value receiver's copy shares the map's storage — mutations of the MAP contents work; only rebinding m itself would need a pointer, and rebinding a map is almost always wrong.\n\nThe consistency rule's deeper reason: a type is either a VALUE or a REFERENCE, conceptually. Mixing receivers breaks the concept — users can't predict copy semantics per method. time.Time says 'value' with every receiver; bytes.Buffer says 'reference' with every one. The receiver kind isn't per-method trivia — it's the type's identity statement, applied uniformly."),
("Does a value receiver protect the receiver from modification?",
"It protects the STRUCT'S FIELDS — writes die in the copy. It does NOT protect data behind reference fields: slice elements, map entries, and pointees write through the shared reference and mutate the caller's data.",
"The precise contract, in two clauses:\n\nClause 1 — the struct itself: safe. A value receiver's u is a copy; u.Name = s, u.Age++ — every field write lands in copy storage, gone at return. The original's fields are untouched. Executed proof: Rename(\"Bo\") through a value receiver left u.Name == \"Al\".\n\nClause 2 — data the struct POINTS to: not safe. The copy is shallow — slice/map/pointer fields share their underlying data with the original:\n\n```go\nfunc (d Doc) AddTag(t string) {\n    d.Tags[0] = t       // writes through the SHARED slice — escapes!\n}\nd := Doc{Tags: []string{\"old\"}}\nd.AddTag(\"new\")\n// d.Tags[0] == \"new\" — the value receiver did NOT protect this\n```\n\nThat's the trap: the author saw a value receiver, concluded 'safe', wrote through a reference field — and the mutation escaped. The receiver kind protects exactly one layer — the field values — and reference fields' VALUES are the headers; the data beyond them is shared by construction.\n\nThe append-counter-trap: d.Tags = append(d.Tags, t) through a value receiver — the append may write into shared capacity (visible!) or allocate (private) — the caller's slice may or may not change depending on capacity. Non-deterministic-looking mutation through a value receiver is almost always this case.\n\nSo the rule for reviewers: value receivers guarantee the receiver's FIELDS are safe; any method writing through d.Slice[i], d.Map[k], or *d.Ptr is mutating shared state regardless of receiver kind — and belongs behind a pointer receiver with a comment saying so.",
"The shallow copy is the mechanism — the spec-level behavior: assignment (including the call-boundary copy into a value receiver) copies the struct's fields. Reference-typed fields are headers (pointer+len for slices; pointer for maps; the pointer for pointers) — the headers copy; the pointees don't. The copy and the original are two structs sharing: one backing array per slice field, one table per map field, one pointee per pointer field.\n\nWrites through the header — d.Tags[0] = x (backing array write), d.Map[k] = v (table write), d.Ptr.Name = y (pointee write) — land in the SHARED structure: visible on both sides. Writes TO the header — d.Tags = append/new-slice, d.Ptr = other, d.Map = nil — rebind the COPY's header: private. The complete rule table for value receivers, and every surprise in this area is a row of it.\n\nThe sync.Mutex corollary — go vet's copylocks check exists because of this: a struct with an embedded mutex has a field whose header-copy doesn't even share correctly — the mutex's internal state (locked bit, waiter list) is VALUE data that must not be duplicated. Copying a locked mutex creates a second mutex claiming the same lock state — undefined behavior of the worst kind. go vet flags every copy: value receiver on a locked-fields struct, value assignment, range-copy — the linter enforces the spec's 'don't\" rule that the compiler can't because it can't know the semantic.\n\nThe deep design point for interviews: Go's protection granularity is deliberately field-level, not object-level. A value receiver isn't const (no C++ const-depth), isn't a defensive copy of the object graph — it's a fast shallow copy whose semantics compose with the value/reference type distinction running through the whole language. Understanding that ONE distinction (headers share, fields don't) predicts receiver behavior, slice behavior, map behavior, and interface boxing — it's the same fact every time."),
("How does the receiver type affect interface satisfaction?",
"Rule: *T's method set includes value AND pointer receivers; T's includes only value receivers. So pointer-receiver methods make the type satisfy interfaces ONLY as the pointer.",
"The rule, then the consequences:\n\n```go\nfunc (u User)  Greet() string\nfunc (u *User) Inc()\n\nvar g Greeter        // interface { Greet() string }\nvar u User\nvar p *User = &u\n\ng = u   // works — Greet is in User's method set (value receiver)\ng = p   // works — *User's set is a SUPERSET\n\nvar inc Incrementer  // interface { Inc() }\ninc = u   // COMPILE ERROR — Inc is not in User's method set\ninc = p   // works — Inc is in *User's method set\n```\n\nThe asymmetry's direction matters: the POINTER can always stand in for the value (its method set includes everything the value's does) — but not the reverse. A pointer-heavy type (all methods on *User) satisfies its interfaces only at pointer type; storing values in interfaces fails at compile time with the method-set error.\n\nThe classic bug shape: a constructor returns User (value), methods are pointer receivers, users try to store in the interface — the compiler names the missing method. The fixes, by idiom: constructors of pointer-receiver types return *User (the standard library's pattern — NewBuffer() *Buffer); or the type moves its methods to value receivers (when mutation isn't needed); or the interface gets satisfied by a wrapper. The first fix is the convention because it makes the whole type's usage uniform — everything holds *User.\n\nThe storage consequence worth naming: interface values hold a copy of what you put in (u boxed into the interface is a snapshot — mutating the original u after doesn't affect the boxed copy; boxing p copies the POINTER — the pointee stays shared). Receiver kinds, method sets, and interface semantics are one system — the receiver choice at type-design time decides the interface story forever."),
("Can you mix value and pointer receivers on the same type?",
"Yes — it compiles. But the method set splits (interface satisfaction differs by which receivers your methods use), and consistency is the strong convention: all value or all pointer once the type needs one pointer method.",
"Legal, discouraged, and the reason is the split method set:\n\n```go\ntype Counter struct{ n int }\nfunc (c Counter)  Get() int  { return c.n }   // value\nfunc (c *Counter) Inc()     { c.n++ }        // pointer\n```\n\nNow the sets: Counter{Get}; *Counter{Get, Inc}. An interface{Get()} takes both; interface{Inc()} takes only *Counter; interface{Get() Inc()} takes only *Counter — a value Counter can never satisfy the full one.\n\nThat split is the maintenance hazard: each new interface that mixes the type's methods silently selects which receiver form works — users discover via compile errors, each interface a fresh surprise. The type 'meant to be' a value OR a reference stops being either; it's whatever each interface admits.\n\nThe convention that prevents it: the FIRST pointer-receiver method (needed for mutation, or size, or mutex) converts the whole type — all methods become pointer receivers. The cost of the rule (value methods that didn't need the pointer pay indirection) is far below the cost of the split (per-interface satisfaction roulette + readers tracking per-method receiver kinds).\n\nThe exceptions the ecosystem accepts: small value-semantics types with ONE odd method (e.g. a Copy() that takes a value receiver for the return). And the standard library exhibit of the uniform rule: time.Time — every method value receiver (immutable value semantics); bytes.Buffer — every method pointer (mutable reference semantics). Each type is one thing, and its receivers announce which.\n\nThe interview phrasing that lands: mixing doesn't break compilation — it breaks predictability. The receiver kind is the type's value-vs-reference identity; a mixed type has two identities, and every interface picks one at random.",
"The method-set split, followed to its compile-time consequence: an interface that mixes methods from both receiver kinds — interface { Get() Inc() } — can only be satisfied by *Counter. The value Counter satisfies Get()-only interfaces, so every place the type is USED chooses a receiver form: the API surface forks per interface. Callers hold Counter where value suffices, *Counter where both are needed — two shapes of the same type in one codebase, decided interface by interface.\n\nWhat consistency buys: one receiver kind means one method set means one interface story. *Counter everywhere: every satisfiable interface is satisfied — by the pointer, uniformly. The uniform type is legible: readers never ask which form satisfies what — the answer is always the pointer.\n\nThe escape hatches that keep the rule honest: types that are genuinely values (small, immutable — time.Time) keep ALL value receivers — consistency costs nothing. Types that need one pointer method convert once, wholesale — the conversion is mechanical (add the star to every receiver), the behavior change is none (value methods on pointers work — the superset rule), and the only observable difference is interface satisfaction, which becomes BETTER. The refactor is safe in one direction — value-to-pointer — and the ecosystem convention reflects that asymmetry."),
("Does Go have inheritance through receivers?",
"No — receivers attach methods to one type; there's no overriding, no virtual dispatch, no subtype from receivers. Embedding promotes methods (reuse) but satisfies interfaces only through promotion — the embedded type's methods remain its own, statically bound.",
"Three claims to test against the language, each executed-verified earlier:\n\nNo overriding: Admin embedding User with User's Greet — Admin gets Greet PROMOTED, but if Admin declares its own Greet, that's SHADOWING, not override: User's Greet still exists, still callable via a.User.Greet(), still what a User-typed variable calls. There's no dispatch layer where a User-typed value 'really' being an Admin behaves differently — Go values carry their type; the type decides the method, statically.\n\nNo subtype: an Admin is not a User — no assignment (var u User = admin fails; the promoted FIELDS don't make the types related), no 'is-a'. The relation is HAS-A (a User field) plus name promotion (syntax sugar over the field).\n\nNo virtual dispatch through receivers: method calls bind at compile time to the receiver's declared type. The ONLY runtime dispatch in Go is interfaces — the interface value carries (type, value), and the method call reads the type's itable. That mechanism belongs to interfaces, not receivers/embedding.\n\nThe positive framing — what Go gives instead: composition with promotion (reuse fields and methods, satisfy interfaces via promotion — the earlier embedded-AppStore demo), plus interfaces as the polymorphism mechanism (small, satisfied implicitly, resolved at runtime). The pair replaces inheritance's two jobs — reuse (embedding) and polymorphism (interfaces) — with orthogonal mechanisms that don't tangle: no fragile base (an embedded type's changes can't break an outer's override), no deep hierarchies (interfaces compose flat), and the type system stays decidable from local declarations.\n\nThe interview close: 'inheritance through receivers' is testing whether you know receivers are attachment, not a class system. The complete answer names embedding as the reuse mechanism, interfaces as the polymorphism mechanism, and states the deliberate split.",
""),
],
}
